<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corippus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Cresconius Corippus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Cresconius Corippus was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet from Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 6th century. His epic Iohannis, in eight books, celebrates the campaigns of the Byzantine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-troglita">John Troglita</a> against the Moorish tribes in the 540s and is a chief source for the Laguatan and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples. He later wrote a panegyric on the accession of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corippus.jpg" alt="Corippus" width="320" height="584" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christophori Plantini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corippi_Africani_grammatici_De_laudibus_Iustini_Augusti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
